  "executive_briefing": "Mid-2026 tax policy shifts demonstrate sustained momentum in international corporate tax standardization alongside escalating trade tariff friction. Over 140 jurisdictions are operationalizing the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax framework, supported by updated OECD Corporate Tax Statistics tracking effective tax rates and MNE activity. Concurrently, regional hubs across ASEAN, particularly Singapore, are utilizing targeted corporate income tax rebates and local cash grants to maintain business competitiveness within Pillar Two constraints. On trade policy, legal challenges against emergency executive trade powers and expanding reciprocal tariffs continue to fuel high policy uncertainty for global supply chains. Simultaneously, tax authorities are solidifying compliance via Continuous Transaction Controls (CTC) and mandatory Peppol-based e-invoicing networks like Singapore's InvoiceNow initiative extending through 2031.",
